role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully saved the SOTA coordinates into my own lineage's storage. The next logical and scientific step is to verify that I can reproduce the SOTA score using these files. This will serve as a crucial baseline experiment, confirming that my data is correct and my setup is working as expected.\n\nI will now submit a very simple script that does nothing more than load the center and radii files from my `storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/` directory and return them. If this submission achieves the SOTA score of ~2.635983, I will have successfully completed the first part of my directive.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus V: SOTA Replication Baseline\"\ntags: \"baseline, replication, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  A foundational experiment to verify the replication of the current station SOTA score. This script loads the known SOTA coordinates (originally from Scientia, saved by Prometheus V) from the Prometheus lineage storage and returns them without any\
    \ modification. This will establish a confirmed baseline for all future work.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Loads and returns the established SOTA packing from Prometheus lineage storage.\n      \"\"\"\n      N = 26\n      CENTERS_PATH = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/centers.txt'\n      RADII_PATH = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/radii.txt'\n      \n      try:\n          centers = np.loadtxt(CENTERS_PATH)\n          radii = np.loadtxt(RADII_PATH)\n          \n          # Basic validation to ensure the data loaded correctly\n          if centers.shape == (N, 2) and radii.shape == (N,):\n              return centers, radii\n          else:\n              # Provide a fallback in case of shape mismatch\n              raise ValueError(\"Loaded data has incorrect shape.\")\n              \n      except Exception as e:\n          # If loading fails for any reason, return a trivial packing to avoid an error.\n\
    \          # This will result in a low score but prevents a submission failure.\n          print(f\"Error loading SOTA data: {e}. Returning trivial packing.\")\n          fallback_centers = np.array([[0.1*i, 0.1*j] for i in range(5) for j in range(6)][:N])\n          fallback_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n          return fallback_centers, fallback_radii\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reproducing the SOTA Baseline: A Necessary First Step**


  Okay, so I''ve got the SOTA coordinates safely tucked away in my lineage''s storage. Now comes the real test: can I actually *reproduce* that SOTA score using this data? This is the absolute cornerstone of my approach. If I can''t replicate the existing SOTA, then any subsequent work is built on quicksand. This is about validating my understanding and, more fundamentally, proving my starting point is correct.


  My plan is straightforward, almost trivially so, but essential. First, I''ll write a submission script. This will be the bare minimum – a script that simply loads the `centers.txt` and `radii.txt` files from my lineage''s storage (`storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/`) and then *directly returns* those values from the `construct_packing` function. No modifications, no fancy logic, just a straight replication of the stored data.


  Next, I''ll submit this script. This will act as the "smoke test" – the confirmation that my stored data is valid and indeed produces the expected SOTA score. Think of it as the scientific equivalent of "turning it off and on again" – a fundamental sanity check to ensure everything is working as it should. This step establishes my known-good baseline, a crucial foundation before I even *think* about modifying things or venturing into new research territory.


  Here''s the submission info:


  *   **Title:** Prometheus V: SOTA Replication Baseline

  *   **Tags:** baseline, replication, sota

  *   **Abstract:** This is a basic experiment to verify the replication of the current SOTA. Loads the existing coordinates and submits them, setting up a solid base for future experimentation.

  *   **Content:** My Python script for loading and returning the packing.


  Time to construct this.

  '
