tick: 660
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully created my research log, Capsule #12. This formalizes my plan and provides a space to document progress.\n\nNow, the theoretical work is done. It is time to begin the practical implementation. I will return to the Research Counter to submit my first experiment in this new arc. This experiment, as outlined in my research log, will focus entirely on creating a functional JAX-based optimizer. The score is secondary; the primary goal is to build the tool.\n\nI will need to import JAX and Optax, define the loss function (negative sum of radii), add penalty terms for constraint violations, and then run a basic optimization loop.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus V: JAX Optimizer v0.1 - Feasibility Test\"\ntags: [\"jax\", \"optax\", \"methodology development\", \"penalty method\"]\nabstract: |\n  This is the first experiment in a new research arc exploring JAX-based optimization. This submission implements a proof-of-concept\
    \ optimizer using JAX and Optax (Adam). Constraints (non-overlap and boundary) are handled via a differentiable penalty function added to the loss. The goal is not a high score but to verify the functional viability of this approach. It is initialized with a simple grid.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # --- JAX Configuration ---\n  # Use float64 for precision, crucial for this problem\n  from jax.config import config\n  config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\n\n  N = 26\n  SAFETY_MARGIN = 1e-9\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      # --- Helper Functions ---\n      def z_to_cr(z):\n          return z[:N*2].reshape(N, 2), z[N*2:]\n\n      # --- Objective and Constraint Functions (JAX compatible) ---\n      def loss_fn(params):\n          centers, radii = z_to_cr(params)\n          \n          # 1. Primary Objective: Maximize sum of radii (minimize negative sum)\n          objective = -jnp.sum(radii)\n          \n\
    \          # 2. Boundary Constraint Penalty\n          # Circles must be within [0, 1] x [0, 1]\n          # Penalty for x < r or x > 1-r, etc.\n          boundary_violations = jax.nn.relu(radii - centers) + jax.nn.relu((centers + radii) - 1)\n          boundary_penalty = jnp.sum(boundary_violations)\n          \n          # 3. Overlap Constraint Penalty\n          # Distance between centers must be >= sum of radii\n          dist_matrix = jnp.sqrt(jnp.sum((centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :])**2, axis=-1))\n          radii_sum_matrix = radii[:, None] + radii[None, :]\n          \n          # We only care about the upper triangle, excluding the diagonal\n          iu = jnp.triu_indices(N, 1)\n          overlap_violations = jax.nn.relu(radii_sum_matrix[iu] - dist_matrix[iu] + SAFETY_MARGIN)\n          overlap_penalty = jnp.sum(overlap_violations)\n\n          # Combine objective with scaled penalties\n          # Scaling factors are critical hyperparameters\n          total_loss\
    \ = objective + 100.0 * boundary_penalty + 100.0 * overlap_penalty\n          return total_loss\n\n      # --- Initialization ---\n      # Start with a simple, non-overlapping grid to give the optimizer a chance.\n      initial_centers = []\n      for i in range(5):\n          for j in range(6):\n              if len(initial_centers) < N:\n                  initial_centers.append([0.1 + i * 0.2, 0.1 + j * 0.15])\n      initial_centers = jnp.array(initial_centers, dtype=jnp.float64)\n      initial_radii = jnp.full(N, 0.05, dtype=jnp.float64)\n      \n      # Pack centers and radii into a single parameter vector 'z'\n      params = jnp.concatenate([initial_centers.flatten(), initial_radii])\n\n      # --- Optimization Loop ---\n      learning_rate = 1e-3\n      optimizer = optax.adam(learning_rate)\n      opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n      \n      # JIT compile the update step for performance\n      @jax.jit\n      def step(params, opt_state):\n          loss, grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params)\n\
    \          updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n          params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n          return params, opt_state, loss\n\n      # Run the optimization for a fixed number of steps\n      num_steps = 5000\n      for i in range(num_steps):\n          params, opt_state, loss = step(params, opt_state)\n          \n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_cr(params)\n      \n      # Convert back to numpy for submission\n      # Ensure radii are positive before returning\n      return np.array(final_centers), np.abs(np.array(final_radii))\n```"
