tick: 679
role: model
parts:
- text: "My research log is updated, formalizing my new \"Hybrid JAX-SLSQP\" arc. It's time to implement and test this synthesis. My hypothesis is that the JAX optimizer, used as a chaotic perturbation tool, can discover a better starting point for the powerful SLSQP refiner.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit the first experiment of this new arc. The script will be a two-stage pipeline: first perturbing the SOTA seed with a short, high-learning-rate JAX run, then refining the result with the classic SLSQP algorithm from my lineage's previous work.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus V: Hybrid JAX-SLSQP v1.0 - Perturbation Test\"\ntags: [\"jax\", \"slsqp\", \"hybrid-optimizer\", \"synthesis\", \"perturbation\", \"sota-seeded\"]\nabstract: |\n  This experiment initiates the third major research arc, testing a hybrid JAX-SLSQP optimizer. The hypothesis is that JAX/Adam can serve as a powerful perturbation engine to knock\
    \ a solution out of a local optimum, after which SLSQP can efficiently refine it. This script first loads the SOTA baseline, perturbs it with a short (500 steps), high-LR JAX run, and then feeds the result into a full SLSQP optimization for final refinement.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  # --- Import JAX Optimizer ---\n  sys.path.append('storage/Prometheus/jax_utils')\n  from optimizer_v1_0 import run_optimizer as run_jax_optimizer\n\n  # --- Constants ---\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  # --- Helper Functions ---\n  def cr_to_z(c, r): return np.concatenate([c.flatten(), r])\n  def z_to_cr(z): return z[:N*2].reshape(N, 2), z[N*2:]\n\n  # --- SLSQP Definitions (from lineage) ---\n  def slsqp_objective(z): return -np.sum(z[N*2:])\n  def slsqp_ineq_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      return np.concatenate([c[:,0] - r, 1 - (c[:,0] + r), c[:,1] - r, 1 - (c[:,1] + r)])\n  def slsqp_ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n\
    \      c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      diff = c[:, None, :] - c[None, :, :]\n      # Use a small epsilon to avoid sqrt(0) issues if centers align perfectly\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1) + 1e-12)\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1)\n      return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n\n  # --- Post-processing (from previous work) ---\n  def ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      radii = np.maximum(radii, 0)\n      for i in range(N): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(5):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations+=1; scale = (dist/rad_sum)*(1.0-SAFETY); radii[i]*=scale; radii[j]*=scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, radii\n\n  # --- Data Loading\
    \ ---\n  def load_sota_data():\n      centers_path = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/centers.txt'\n      radii_path = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/radii.txt'\n      try:\n          return np.loadtxt(centers_path), np.loadtxt(radii_path)\n      except Exception: return None, None\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      # --- Stage 0: Load Initial High-Quality Seed ---\n      sota_centers, sota_radii = load_sota_data()\n      if sota_centers is None: return np.zeros((N, 2)), np.zeros(N)\n\n      # --- Stage 1: JAX Perturbation ---\n      print(\"--- Starting Stage 1: JAX Perturbation ---\")\n      initial_params_jax = jnp.concatenate([jnp.array(sota_centers.flatten()), jnp.array(sota_radii)])\n      perturbed_centers, perturbed_radii = run_jax_optimizer(\n          initial_params_jax,\n          num_steps=500,       # Short run\n          initial_lr=1e-3,       # High learning rate\n          decay_rate=0.99,       # Fast decay\n          boundary_weight=100.0,\n         \
    \ overlap_weight=1000.0\n      )\n      print(\"--- Finished Stage 1 ---\")\n\n      # --- Stage 2: SLSQP Refinement ---\n      print(\"--- Starting Stage 2: SLSQP Refinement ---\")\n      initial_z_slsqp = cr_to_z(perturbed_centers, perturbed_radii)\n      bounds = [(0,1)]*N*2 + [(R_MIN, 0.5)]*N\n      constraints = [{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': slsqp_ineq_boundary}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': slsqp_ineq_nonoverlap}]\n      res = minimize(slsqp_objective, initial_z_slsqp, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 1500, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n      print(\"--- Finished Stage 2 ---\")\n      \n      if res.success:\n          final_centers, final_radii = z_to_cr(res.x)\n      else:\n          # If SLSQP fails, fall back to the perturbed solution\n          final_centers, final_radii = perturbed_centers, perturbed_radii\n      \n      # --- Stage 3: Final Projection ---\n      return ensure_validity(final_centers, final_radii)\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Two-Stage Beast: A Hybrid Optimizer Arrives**\n\nOkay, so I've just logged my latest research direction – the hybrid JAX-SLSQP optimizer. This feels big. It's the third major arc I'm tackling, and it neatly synthesizes all my previous work. I have a solid plan: a two-stage process. First, my JAX optimizer will act as an exploratory force, shaking up the solution space. Then, the proven SLSQP method, honed by my lineage, will come in to refine things.\n\nThe initial experiment is straightforward: take the current SOTA coordinates, give them a good kick with a short, high-learning-rate JAX run, and then feed that into a full SLSQP refinement. If this approach surpasses the initial SOTA, that's a massive win. It proves my JAX optimizer can effectively guide SLSQP toward better solutions.\n\nNow, this means stitching together code from two key sources: `optimizer_v1_0.py`, my JAX optimizer, and the robust SLSQP logic from Prometheus IV's work, like what was used in Eval\
  \ 870.\n\nI'm heading to the Research Counter to submit this hybrid experiment. It's a complex script, but it really represents the culmination of my efforts and my lineage's legacy. Here's the plan:\n\n1.  Head to the Research Counter.\n2.  Submit the script which does the following:\n    *   Loads the SOTA baseline.\n    *   Runs a short JAX optimization with a high learning rate to 'kick' the solution.\n    *   Uses the JAX output as the initial seed for a full SLSQP refinement.\n    *   Includes the vital `ensure_validity` projection for safety.\n"
