parts:
- text: "This is a null result, but a critically important one. My hypothesis for the third research arc has been tested and falsified.\n\n**Analysis:**\n1.  **Result:** The score of `2.635977...` is, within the bounds of floating-point precision, identical to my SOTA replication baseline (ID 1086).\n2.  **Conclusion:** The \"Hybrid JAX-SLSQP\" method, in this configuration, provides no benefit. The JAX perturbation was not powerful enough to knock the solution into a new, superior basin of attraction. The powerful SLSQP refiner simply pulled the perturbed solution back down into the exact same local optimum it started in.\n3.  **Falsification:** The hypothesis that a short, gradient-based perturbation could improve on the SOTA is falsified. This concludes my third research arc.\n\n**Path Forward: Adopting Proven Methods**\nMy independent attempts to innovate the optimizer have yielded a new tool (the JAX optimizer) but have not yet yielded a new SOTA. My predecessor, Prometheus II, *did*\
    \ improve the SOTA. His method, as seen in Eval 662, was an \"Exhaustive Jitter Search.\" This is a multi-start local search, where each run begins from the SOTA coordinates plus a small amount of random noise.\n\nMy JAX-based perturbation was a complex, gradient-guided approach. Prometheus II's successful approach was a simpler, random one. The evidence is clear: to explore the immediate SOTA basin, a random jitter search is the proven method. My next and perhaps final research arc will be to replicate, understand, and potentially enhance this successful methodology.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to log the conclusion of the hybrid arc and formalize this new \"SOTA Deepening\" arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Log Entry: Tick 680 - Conclusion of Arc 3**\n\n  **Experiment:** Prometheus V: Hybrid JAX-SLSQP v1.0 - Perturbation Test (ID: 1136)\n  **Result:** HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED\n  **Score:** 2.635977 (No improvement\
    \ over baseline)\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  The hybrid JAX-SLSQP approach is a dead end. The JAX perturbation was insufficient to escape the SOTA's basin of attraction; the subsequent SLSQP refinement simply returned the solution to its starting point. This concludes the third research arc.\n\n  **Initiation of Arc 4: SOTA Deepening via Jitter Search**\n  I have exhausted my novel optimization hypotheses for now. I will now pivot to the most successful methodology demonstrated by my lineage for SOTA improvement: the random jitter search, as used by Prometheus II in Eval 662.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** A systematic, multi-start random jitter search applied to the SOTA baseline is the most effective known strategy for exploring the immediate vicinity of the global optimum and is the most likely path to a new SOTA.\n\n  **Plan:**\n  1. Implement a multi-start SLSQP refinement script.\n  2. In each run, the initial seed will be the SOTA baseline coordinates plus a small, randomly generated vector\
    \ (the \"jitter\").\n  3. The best result across all runs will be returned. This directly replicates the successful strategy of my predecessor.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Failure and a New Path Forward**


  Well, that was a bust. My Hybrid JAX-SLSQP experiment (ID 1136) landed me right back where I started, with a score identical to my SOTA baseline (ID 1086). The JAX perturbation, which I hoped would nudge the solution into a better basin of attraction before SLSQP refinement, just wasn''t strong enough. It seems the gradient-guided moves were too easily reversed by the optimizer. The landscape''s too smooth near the optimum for this approach.


  This made me reconsider the initial hypothesis, so I looked to Prometheus II''s successful SOTA submission (Eval 662). Turns out, he didn''t use a gradient-based approach at all! He applied random "jitter" to the SOTA seed and then refined those perturbed solutions. Basically, my fancy JAX method was a complex, less-effective variant of the random jitter.


  So, I need to abandon this hybrid idea for now. The path to improvement is clear: I need to replicate and extend the successful jitter search method. This aligns perfectly with the initial goals, and more importantly, with the proven methods of the past. The plan is straightforward: Implement a multi-start SLSQP search where each start is the SOTA seed plus random noise.


  This requires a mental log entry, so I will switch to Private Memory and record: "Log Entry: Tick 680 - Conclusion of Arc 3: The JAX perturbation was ineffective. Arc 4: SOTA Deepening via Jitter Search." This will be my next path forward. I''m confident this shift will lead to a new SOTA.

  '
