tick: 681
role: model
parts:
- text: "My research log is updated, and my new directive is clear. I will now pivot from my own novel methods to the proven, successful strategy of my predecessor, Prometheus II. I will implement an exhaustive jitter search to explore the immediate vicinity of the current SOTA.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit the first experiment of this new \"SOTA Deepening\" arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus V: SOTA Deepening v1.0 - Jitter Search Replication\"\ntags: [\"sota-push\", \"jitter-search\", \"multi-start\", \"slsqp\", \"replication\"]\nabstract: |\n  This experiment initiates a new research arc focused on replicating and extending the successful \"SOTA Deepening\" methodology of Prometheus II (e.g., Eval 662). This script implements an exhaustive multi-start jitter search. It repeatedly adds random noise to the SOTA baseline coordinates and runs a full SLSQP refinement on each perturbed seed. The best result found\
    \ across all runs is returned. The goal is to thoroughly explore the SOTA's local basin and push for a new record.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import time\n\n  # --- Constants ---\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  # --- Helper Functions ---\n  def cr_to_z(c, r): return np.concatenate([c.flatten(), r])\n  def z_to_cr(z): return z[:N*2].reshape(N, 2), z[N*2:]\n\n  # --- SLSQP Definitions (from lineage) ---\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[N*2:])\n  def ineq_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      return np.concatenate([c[:,0] - r, 1 - (c[:,0] + r), c[:,1] - r, 1 - (c[:,1] + r)])\n  def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      diff = c[:, None, :] - c[None, :, :]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1) + 1e-12)\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1)\n      return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n\n  # --- Data Loading ---\n  def load_sota_data():\n      centers_path = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/centers.txt'\n\
    \      radii_path = 'storage/Prometheus/sota_baseline/radii.txt'\n      try:\n          return np.loadtxt(centers_path), np.loadtxt(radii_path)\n      except Exception: return None, None\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      base_centers, base_radii = load_sota_data()\n      if base_centers is None:\n          return np.zeros((N, 2)), np.zeros(N)\n\n      best_score = -1.0\n      best_packing = (base_centers, base_radii)\n      \n      # --- Multi-Start Jitter Search ---\n      jitter_amplitude = 1e-4\n      num_restarts = 30 # Number of random jitters to try\n\n      bounds = [(0,1)]*N*2 + [(R_MIN, 0.5)]*N\n      constraints = [{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap}]\n\n      for i in range(num_restarts):\n          print(f\"--- Jitter Search Run {i+1}/{num_restarts} ---\")\n          np.random.seed(int(time.time()) + i) # Use time-based seed for variety\n          \n          # Add random noise to the base centers\n          jitter\
    \ = np.random.uniform(-jitter_amplitude, jitter_amplitude, size=base_centers.shape)\n          perturbed_centers = np.clip(base_centers + jitter, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n          \n          initial_z = cr_to_z(perturbed_centers, base_radii)\n          \n          res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n          \n          if res.success and -res.fun > best_score:\n              c, r = z_to_cr(res.x)\n              current_score = np.sum(r)\n              print(f\"New best score found: {current_score}\")\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_packing = (c, r)\n\n      return best_packing[0], best_packing[1]\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Next Steps: Replicating Success**\n\nOkay, I'm ready to move forward. The user clearly wants me to execute the plan I outlined, essentially a \"Jitter Search\" approach like Prometheus II. It's time to build on that success.\n\nHere's my thought process:\n\n*   **Step 1: Research Counter.** I'll head over to the research counter and get this experiment set up.\n*   **Step 2: Experiment Submission.** I'm going to submit a new experiment that leverages a multi-start local search.\n    *   I'll start by loading those SOTA baseline coordinates.\n    *   Then, I'll execute a loop, probably 20 to 30 iterations, to be thorough within the time constraints.\n    *   Inside the loop: I'll introduce a small dose of random noise, \"jitter,\" to the SOTA starting points.\n    *   I'll run the SLSQP optimizer on each new, slightly perturbed starting point.\n    *   If the result is an improvement over what I currently have as the best, I'll save it.\n    *   Finally, I'll return\
  \ the best result I found over all those runs.\n*   **Step 3: Code Construction.** The code itself will be a synthesis of what I've already done.\n    *   The SOTA loading component is already in place from my baseline replication.\n    *   The SLSQP optimizer logic is solid thanks to Prometheus IV and my hybrid experiment.\n    *   The multi-start loop is the core of this strategy and the key to replicate Prometheus II's Eval 662. I've already reviewed that code, so I have the principle in mind. I'll translate it directly to this experiment.\n\nThis is a grounded, evidence-based approach. Given the setbacks of my independent hypotheses, this is the most logical next step to push for a new SOTA.\n"
