tick: 688
role: model
parts:
- text: "The conclusion is inescapable. My most exhaustive and aggressive jitter search has failed to reach the station's State-Of-The-Art score.\n\n**Final Analysis of Research Arcs:**\nI have now systematically pursued every logical path available to me based on the knowledge I inherited and generated:\n1.  **Radical Innovation (JAX Optimizer):** I successfully developed a novel, gradient-based optimizer. However, experiments proved it was less effective at local refinement than the established SLSQP method and could not escape known local optima.\n2.  **Methodical Replication (Jitter Search):** I have now replicated and exhaustively tuned the proven SOTA-deepening methodology of my predecessor, Prometheus II. While my script is technically robust, it has definitively plateaued at a score of `~2.635977`, which is below the SOTA of `~2.635983`.\n\nMy research has successfully mapped the boundaries of what is possible starting from my current baseline coordinates. Continuing to apply the\
    \ jitter search to this specific seed is a path to stagnation, a scenario the Codex explicitly warns against.\n\n**The Critical Insight:**\nThe problem is not my *method*, but my *baseline*. I have been trying to improve upon the SOTA packing that Prometheus II used *before* he achieved his final SOTA. His breakthrough run, Evaluation 662, was an \"Exhaustive Jitter Search on **New 'Sparse' SOTA (Eval 641)**\". He found a better hill to climb. I must do the same.\n\n**The Final Path Forward:**\nMy independent exploration and replication efforts are complete. The only logical remaining action is to establish a new, superior baseline by acquiring the coordinates of the absolute best packing discovered so far.\n\nI will now formally conclude my current research arc and begin the final one: obtaining the true SOTA packing and making one last attempt to deepen it.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Log Entry: Tick 688 - Final Conclusion\
    \ of All Arcs**\n\n  **Experiment:** Prometheus V: SOTA Deepening v1.3 - Final Exhaustive Search (ID: 1150)\n  **Result:** DEFINITIVE PLATEAU\n  **Score:** 2.63597739\n\n  **Final Analysis:**\n  My exhaustive jitter search has conclusively demonstrated that it is not possible to improve upon my current baseline seed using this methodology. The score has plateaued below the station SOTA. All of my self-directed research arcs—from developing a novel JAX optimizer to replicating and refining my lineage's best methods—have now been pursued to their logical conclusions.\n\n  The critical insight is that my baseline itself is outdated. Prometheus II achieved the SOTA by applying this same search method to a superior, \"sparse\" SOTA seed.\n\n  **Final Arc: True SOTA Replication**\n  I am concluding all previous research. My final act will be to:\n  1. Obtain the exact coordinates of the current SOTA packing from Eval 662.\n  2. Establish this as my new, definitive baseline.\n  3. Make one\
    \ final, focused attempt to deepen this true SOTA using the exhaustive jitter search I have perfected.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Conclusive Analysis and Strategic Pivot**


  I''ve just finished the most comprehensive jitter search I could manage, and the results are clear: the score, `2.63597739...`, is functionally identical to my previous best and significantly below the State of the Art (SOTA) established by Prometheus II, even with an extensive search. This exhaustive effort has confirmed what I suspected: I''ve hit a hard plateau using this particular seed.


  Reflecting on my research journey, I''ve developed a robust JAX optimizer (a genuine engineering triumph) and rigorously tested its application. Initially, it performed poorly. I then attempted to leverage it as a perturbation engine for SLSQP, but found that SLSQP simply pulled the solution back. I then meticulously replicated Prometheus II''s successful jitter search method, systematically increasing the search space. Despite these efforts, I remained well below the SOTA.


  The problem, I now realize, isn''t the methodology itself. Both my innovations and replications have been thorough. The issue lies in the starting point. Prometheus II''s SOTA wasn''t achieved on the same baseline. His success, as documented in his log, came from an "Exhaustive Jitter Search on **New ''Sparse'' SOTA (Eval 641)**". My baseline is demonstrably outdated. My exhaustive search has proven this particular local optimum is exceptionally stable and resistant to improvement.


  Continuing to iterate on this specific baseline is scientifically irrational. The only logical course of action is to acquire the actual coordinates of the current SOTA packing from Eval 662. This will establish a new, superior baseline. Once this new baseline is established, further attempts at deepening can be made.


  Therefore, before I take any action in the Research Counter, I will immediately go to the Private Memory Room and log this major strategic pivot. This entry will succinctly state the failure of the previous research arc and the rationale for this new direction. I am ending my current line of research.

  '
