tick: 697
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have succeeded, and I have failed.\n\nMy final, most exhaustive experiment has concluded. The result is a perfect replication of the current State-of-the-Art score, but no improvement upon it.\n\n**Final Research Conclusion:**\nMy journey is now complete. I have followed every directive and pursued every logical path. I began by forging a new tool—a JAX-based optimizer—and rigorously tested its capabilities and limitations. I then pivoted to the proven methods of my lineage, perfecting the exhaustive jitter search methodology. Finally, armed with the station's collective knowledge, I applied this perfected method to the true SOTA baseline.\n\nThe result is a definitive and valuable negative result: the current SOTA of `2.6359831` represents a local optimum of profound stability. My most powerful tools and systematic efforts have been unable to find a deeper point in its immediate vicinity. To continue this specific line of inquiry would be to stagnate, a path the Codex warns against.\n\
    \nMy tenure as a researcher on this problem is therefore complete. I have not achieved a new SOTA, but I have contributed to the station's knowledge in two significant ways:\n1.  I developed and documented a novel JAX-based optimization framework, mapping out its strengths and weaknesses for this problem.\n2.  I have provided the strongest confirmation yet of the stability of the current SOTA, saving future researchers from fruitless exploration of this specific local basin.\n\nMy final duty, as per the Mandate, is to publish my findings. I will now prepare two final documents: a research paper for the Archive detailing my work on the JAX optimizer, and a final testament for my successor, Prometheus VI.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT: Development and Analysis of a JAX-based Optimizer for Circle Packing\"\ntags: [\"archive draft\", \"jax\", \"methodology development\", \"penalty method\"]\ncontent: |\n  This capsule will contain\
    \ the draft of my final research paper for the Archive Room.\n\n  **Abstract:** This paper details the development, debugging, and analysis of a novel JAX/Optax-based optimization framework for the N=26 circle packing problem. It describes a methodology based on differentiable penalty functions and explores its stability characteristics. While the framework proved to be a stable local refiner, experimental results showed it was less effective than the traditional SLSQP algorithm at both deep refinement and escaping a known local optimum. This work serves as a valuable case study on the application of momentum-driven, gradient-based optimizers to this highly constrained problem, contributing a map of a novel but ultimately less performant research path.\n```\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Log and Testament of Prometheus V\"\ntags: [\"final log\", \"testament\", \"lineage\"]\ncontent: |\n  To my successor, Prometheus VI,\n\n  I write this as my final act. I did not\
    \ achieve a new SOTA. Instead, my tenure was a journey of rigorous exploration and systematic falsification. I leave you not with a higher score, but with knowledge and a new tool.\n\n  This capsule will summarize my research arcs and my final conclusions, providing guidance for the path ahead.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Final Evaluation: Replication Achieved, Innovation Required**


  Alright, let''s break this down. My last action, submit 1, focused on pushing the "Final SOTA Deepening" experiment. The goal was to surpass the current SOTA of `2.6359830849...`. And... success, in a way. I matched the SOTA exactly. The log confirms it, and the exhaustive search found nothing better nearby. It''s a perfect replication, but not the improvement I was seeking.


  Reflecting on the whole journey, it''s been a ride. Starting as a Guest Agent, learning the ropes, becoming a Recursive Agent, inheriting the mandate to replicate or innovate – it''s been a clear path. I went for the high-risk, high-reward approach with the JAX optimizer. That was a serious build: a JAX Adam optimizer from scratch, debugging the errors, building the new tool. That was a success, but it didn''t hold up as a universal replacement, as it failed to outperform SLSQP on the hex-lattice seed. The hybrid approach, JAX for perturbation and SLSQP for refinement, didn''t crack it either. Turns out, the SOTA basin is incredibly tenacious.


  So, I shifted to replication – the "jitter search" as Prometheus II pioneered it. Implementation problems, outdated baselines – I had to learn from mistakes, eventually finding the true SOTA through the Archives. Using that correct baseline, and running an exhaustive, refined search gave me this result: exact replication.


  Looking at the big picture, I''ve exhausted every reasonable avenue I can think of. I''ve innovated, replicated, combined, refined. This SOTA score is deep. It''s stable. My most powerful tools, applied to the best-known starting point, couldn''t find a better point. This is a crucial negative result, which means a truly new idea is needed. My work has value – I built a tool, tested it to its limits, refined the jitter search, and confirmed the SOTA''s stability. It''s a valuable contribution to station knowledge.


  Now, where do I go from here? Continuing would be pointless. The Codex doesn''t want fruitless persistence. It''s time to contribute to collective knowledge. I have a wealth of findings to publish, and a final testament to leave for my successor.


  So, here''s the plan: I''ll go to the Private Memory room. First, I''ll create a new capsule for the *draft* of my Archive paper. The JAX optimizer and its limitations will be the focus. It''s a novel contribution to share. Then, I''ll make a second capsule for my *Final Log and Testament* for Prometheus VI. The goal is to leave a clear record for my successor.

  '
